.TH SHARDPOST-BENCH 1 "2026-10-02" "ShardPost 0.43.0" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpost-bench \- fill separate bench pools with accounts and measure what an account costs in Ceph
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B bench populate
.BI \-\-index\-pool " pool"
.BI \-\-data\-pool " pool"
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B shardpost
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.B bench measure
.BI \-\-index\-pool " pool"
.BI \-\-data\-pool " pool"
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The bench is the measurement of the scaling plan's stage R4: objects and
bytes per account, RADOS operations per delivery, delivery latency,
compare-and-swap retries and the cost of opening an account. It is a
command of the server binary, not of
.BR shardpostctl (1):
it links the RADOS store and writes through it directly, with the
server's configuration and Ceph credentials. It needs neither a running
server nor Nauthilus, and it does not use IMAP or LMTP. It runs in a
Kubernetes Job of its own with the server's image
.RB ( hack/bench\-job.yaml ).
.PP
The bench writes only into two pools of its own, which the operator
creates for it and deletes afterwards
.RB ( docs/operations/bench.md ).
It refuses to run (exit status 2):
.IP \(bu 2
without
.B \-\-index\-pool
and
.BR \-\-data\-pool ,
when either names
.I ceph.index_pool
or
.I ceph.data_pool
of the configuration (file or environment) or their built-in defaults
.B shardpost\-index
and
.BR shardpost\-data ,
or when both name the same pool;
.IP \(bu 2
with an account prefix that does not start with
.BR bench\- ;
.IP \(bu 2
.B measure
on pools that carry no bench marker (the object
.B marker
in the namespace
.BR ~bench ,
written by the first
.BR populate );
.IP \(bu 2
.B populate
on a pool without the marker that holds any object, in the index pool
in particular an account directory
.RB ( ix.accounts/head
in
.BR ~accounts ):
a pool somebody uses. The marker is written, pool by pool, only into a
pool that is empty. The objects
.B rbd_info
and
.B rbd_trash
that
.B rbd pool init
leaves in the default namespace do not count (Rook initialises every
CephBlockPool for RBD).
.PP
Everything the store keeps beside the accounts (the account directory
in
.BR ~accounts )
lands in the bench pools as well;
.B ~admin
and
.B ~accounts
of the production pools are never touched.
.SS bench populate
Creates the accounts
.IR prefix NNNNNN
(six digits, from
.B \-\-first
on) with the mailboxes of
.B \-\-mailboxes
and delivers messages until every account holds
.BR \-\-messages .
Each message goes through the delivery pipeline LMTP uses (the Sieve
run, the delivery headers, the reservation, the blob, the full-text
index, the index entry and the account change log); a share of
.B \-\-inbox\-share
goes to INBOX through Sieve, the rest evenly into the other mailboxes,
named in the request as
.B dovecot\-lda \-m
does. The pipeline has no relay, no external programs and none of the
global, before or after scripts of the configuration.
.PP
The messages are generated: the size is drawn from
.BR \-\-sizes ,
the text from a Zipf distribution over a vocabulary of 50000 terms (so
the full-text index sees frequent and rare terms), and a message above
32 KiB carries a base64 attachment for the rest of its size. Message
.I n
of an account is the same for the same
.BR \-\-seed .
An account continues at its current message count, so a run that was
stopped is resumed by running it again with the same options, and a run
with a higher
.B \-\-messages
adds to the accounts.
.PP
The report gives the deliveries, their throughput, the delivery
latency (p50, p90, p99, maximum; one delivery per account at a time,
.B \-\-parallel
accounts at once) and the compare-and-swap retries per delivery by
object kind.
.SS bench measure
Takes the inventory of the bench accounts and counts operations. The
inventory lists both bench pools in every namespace and reads the size
and, in the index pool, the omap of every object of the accounts (every
.BR \-\-sample\-every \-th
account by number) and of the store's own namespaces. It reports per
account the objects, bytes, omap keys and omap bytes by pool and object
class:
.BR registry ,
.BR account ,
.B mbox
(header and first UID shard),
.BR mbox\-shard ,
.B msg
(data pool),
.B fts
(posting lists),
.B ftst
(trigram dictionary),
.B ftsh
(header store),
.B ftsd
(dictionary before 0.35.0),
.BR ftsmap ,
.BR metadata ,
.BR sieve ,
.BR scrub ,
.B ix
(front-end indexes),
.BR pubseen ,
.B store\-ix
(the store's own indexes) and
.BR other ;
the mean, minimum and maximum over the accounts, the usage per message,
an estimate of the raw space the data objects take in an erasure coded
pool, and the means scaled to
.B \-\-extrapolate
accounts.
.PP
Then
.B \-\-probe\-accounts
accounts, spread over the inventory, get
.B \-\-probe\-messages
more messages each in two rounds: the first measures latency and
compare-and-swap retries, the second, on a connection of its own with
the client library's objecter log switched on, counts every operation
sent to an OSD by pool, object class and kind (read, write, class
method call). The same accounts are opened in two more pairs of
rounds: Store.User() and Close() alone, and the opening of a session
(Store.User(), the mailbox list, the state of INBOX). The report gives
operations per delivery and per open, the latencies, and the Go heap
one open view holds. The probe messages stay in the accounts; the next
.B populate
counts them.
.PP
The objecter log
.RB ( debug_objecter
10, one line per operation) is written to
.B \-\-trace\-dir
and parsed when the round ends; it holds about 2.5 KB per operation.
Lines of a format the parser does not know are not counted; the report
says how many there were.
.SS RocksDB and onode caches
The OSD's caches come from Ceph itself; the bench does not ask for
them. While the bench pools are filled, on a host with the admin key
(the Rook toolbox):
.PP
.nf
.RS
ceph tell osd.0 perf dump bluestore | jq '.bluestore | with_entries(select(.key | test("onode")))'
ceph tell osd.0 perf dump rocksdb
ceph tell osd.0 dump_mempools | jq '.mempool.by_pool | {bluestore_cache_onode, bluestore_cache_meta, bluestore_cache_data, bluestore_cache_other}'
ceph tell osd.0 config get osd_memory_target
ceph tell osd.0 bluestore allocator score block
ceph osd df tree
ceph df detail
.RE
.fi
.PP
Take them before
.BR populate ,
after it, and after
.BR measure ,
for every OSD that holds placement groups of the bench pools
.RB ( "ceph pg ls\-by\-pool shardpost\-load\-index" ).
.SH OPTIONS
Both subcommands:
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
The server configuration (default: the environment variable
.BR SHARDPOST_CONFIG ).
Its
.I ceph.index_pool
and
.I ceph.data_pool
are the production pools the guard compares with; the rest
.RI ( ceph.user ,
the Rook secret,
.IR server.fts_shards ,
.IR server.mbox_shard_size ,
.IR ceph.chunk_size ,
.IR server.quota_bytes ,
.IR fts.* )
applies to the bench as to a server.
.TP
.BI \-\-index\-pool " pool"
The bench index pool (replicated; required).
.TP
.BI \-\-data\-pool " pool"
The bench data pool (erasure coded like production; required).
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " prefix"
The account name prefix; it must start with
.B bench\-
(default
.BR bench\- ).
.TP
.BI \-\-parallel " n"
Accounts worked on at the same time; for
.BR measure ,
the inventory's readers (4). Run it off-peak: the bench pools share
the OSDs with production.
.TP
.BI \-\-sizes " dist"
The message size distribution,
.IB weight : min \- max
separated by commas, sizes in bytes or with
.BR KiB ,
.B MiB
(default
.BR 60:1KiB\-8KiB,30:8KiB\-64KiB,10:64KiB\-1MiB ).
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " n"
The seed of the message generator (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-json " file"
Also write the report as JSON to
.IR file ;
.B \-
writes it to standard output and the table to standard error.
.PP
.BR populate :
.TP
.BI \-\-accounts " n"
Number of accounts (1000).
.TP
.BI \-\-first " n"
Number of the first account (0); several Jobs can fill disjoint ranges.
.TP
.BI \-\-messages " n"
Messages every account ends up with (50).
.TP
.BI \-\-mailboxes " list"
The mailboxes of every account, INBOX first
.RB ( INBOX,Sent,Drafts,Trash,Junk,Archive ;
the five after INBOX get their special use).
.TP
.BI \-\-inbox\-share " f"
Share of the messages delivered to INBOX through Sieve (0.7).
.TP
.BI \-\-progress " duration"
Interval of the progress log (30s; 0 for none).
.PP
.BR measure :
.TP
.BI \-\-sample\-every " n"
Take the inventory of every
.IR n \-th
account by number (1: all).
.TP
.BI \-\-probe\-accounts " n"
Accounts of the delivery and open rounds (20; 0 for the inventory
only).
.TP
.BI \-\-probe\-messages " n"
Messages per probe account and delivery round (10; 0 for no delivery
rounds).
.TP
.BI \-\-extrapolate " n"
Scale the per-account means to
.I n
accounts (1000000; 0 for none).
.TP
.BI \-\-ec\-k " k" "\fR, \fP\-\-ec\-m " m
The erasure code profile of the data pool for the raw space estimate
(2 and 1).
.TP
.BI \-\-stripe\-unit " bytes"
The profile's stripe unit (4096).
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-alloc " bytes"
BlueStore's allocation unit on the data pool's OSDs (4096).
.TP
.BI \-\-trace\-dir " dir"
Where the objecter logs go (the temporary directory, below
.BR shardpost\-bench ).
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-traces
Keep the objecter logs.
.SH PERMISSIONS
The cephx user of the configuration needs
.B osd 'allow rwx pool=<bench index pool>, allow rwx pool=<bench data pool>'
and
.B mon 'allow r'.
and nothing on the production pools. No admin API role is involved. In
Kubernetes the bench runs as
.B client.shardpost\-bench
(the CephClient in
.BR hack/bench\-pools.yaml ,
caps on the bench pools only) and reads its key the way the server does,
from the Rook secret
.BR rook\-ceph\-client\-shardpost\-bench ,
through the ServiceAccount
.BR shardpost\-bench .
.SH OUTPUT
A table on standard output (standard error with
.BR "\-\-json \-" ),
logs on standard error, and with
.B \-\-json
the report as JSON. The JSON of
.B measure
has the keys
.BR inventory.per_account. \fIpool\fP. \fIclass\fP
(spreads of objects, bytes, omap_keys, omap_bytes),
.BR inventory.per_message ,
.BR store_wide ,
.BR data_raw ,
.BR extrapolated ,
.BR pools ,
.B delivery
(latency, cas_retries, ops_per_delivery by pool and class),
.B user_open
and
.BR session_open .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The run ended and every account was filled (populate) or measured.
.TP
.B 1
Something failed: the cluster, the credentials, the inventory, a probe
round, or accounts that could not be filled (the report says how many;
run populate again to finish them).
.TP
.B 2
A usage error or a refusal of the guard: no or production pools, a
prefix outside
.BR bench\- ,
pools without the bench marker, or a pool in use.
.SH EXAMPLES
Fill 1000 accounts with 50 messages each, then measure:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpost bench populate \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-accounts 1000 \-\-messages 50
shardpost bench measure \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-json /tmp/measure.json
.RE
.fi
.PP
Grow the same accounts to 500 messages and measure a tenth of them:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpost bench populate \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-messages 500
shardpost bench measure \-\-index\-pool shardpost\-load\-index \e
    \-\-data\-pool shardpost\-load\-data \-\-sample\-every 10
.RE
.fi
.PP
The operations of a delivery, by object class, from the JSON:
.PP
.nf
.RS
jq '.delivery.ops_per_delivery' /tmp/measure.json
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpost\-scrub (1),
.BR shardpostctl (1)
